Wholewheat spaghetti works in place of linguine; it takes a couple of minutes longer.
From your Pantry you''ll likely need: pine nuts (not stocked), garlic and fresh chilli (both Out), and fennel (Low).', array['Start the sauce. Boil the kettle. Grate the onion and garlic into a frying pan over medium-high heat with the oil from the sardine tin. Add the finely sliced parsley stalks (keep the leaves), half the sliced chilli, the capers, a splash of their brine and 100ml water. Cook for 8 minutes, stirring often.', 'Cook the pasta. Boil the linguine in salted water for 2 minutes less than the packet says.', 'Make the salad. Shave the fennel thinly with a speed peeler, keeping any fronds. Toss with the parsley leaves and the rest of the chilli, then grate over the lemon zest.', 'Add sardines and pine nuts. Break the sardines into the onion pan, add the pine nuts and cook gently, stirring, until the pasta is ready.', 'Bring it together. Squeeze in half the lemon juice, then use tongs to move the linguine into the pan. Add enough pasta water to make a silky sauce and cook for 2 more minutes. Season and serve with the fennel salad and lemon wedges.']::text[], '41472e027fa881f0ae90f509e3c93a96'),
('house-black-daal', 'House Black Daal', '🫘', 'Dishoom''s signature creamy black daal (their take on daal makhani). Slow: 4–5 hours, mostly simmering and stirring. Serves 8–10. Source: Dishoom, p.214', 8, 240, 'Dishoom, p.214', null, 'The two steps that matter most: cook the grains fully in step 2, and watch the pot closely in step 5.
When reheating leftovers, loosen with cream or cream and water, not just water.', array['Rinse the daal. Cover with water in a large bowl, whisk briefly, let it settle and pour off the water. Repeat 3–4 times until the water runs clear.', 'Cook the grains. Put the daal in a large saucepan with at least 4 litres of cold water. Boil steadily for 2–3 hours, skimming off any scum and topping up with boiling water so the grains stay well covered. They''re done when completely soft, skins coming away, and the white inside is creamy (not crumbly) when pressed. Turn off the heat and leave for 15 minutes.', 'Make the paste. Mix the garlic and ginger pastes, tomato purée, salt, chilli powder and garam masala.', 'Cook hard. Drain the daal, then cover by 3–4cm with freshly boiled water. Bring to the boil over medium-high heat, add the paste and butter, and cook rapidly for 30 minutes, stirring regularly so it doesn''t stick.', 'Simmer until creamy. Lower the heat and simmer for 1–1½ hours, stirring very often. Add a little boiling water whenever the liquid gets close to the level of the grains. The creaminess should come from the grains breaking down into the liquid, not from letting the water boil off.', 'Finish. Add the cream, cook for another 15 minutes, and serve with chapatis or other Indian breads.']::text[], '41372e027fa8815ba39dff4338a00595'),
('smoky-spanish-chicken-chorizo-potato-and-cannellini-bean-stew', 'Smoky Spanish Chicken, Chorizo, Potato and Cannellini Bean Stew', '🍲', 'Paprika-and-garlic rubbed chicken thighs simmered with crisp chorizo, baby potatoes, tomatoes and cannellini beans. One pot, on the hob. Serves 4, about 1 hour 15 minutes', 4, 75, null, null, 'The water stands in for stock; the chorizo, paprika and tomato carry the flavour, and the potatoes need liquid to cook in.
Boneless thighs work too; cut the final simmer to about 15 minutes.
Freezes well for up to 3 months. Loosen with a splash of water when reheating.
Optional extras: a splash of red wine or sherry with the tomatoes, roasted red peppers from a jar, a handful of spinach wilted in at the end.', array['Season the chicken. Toss the thighs with the paprika, garlic, chilli flakes, salt, pepper and a drizzle of oil. Leave for 15 minutes, or overnight in the fridge.', 'Sear the chicken. Heat the oil in a large casserole pot over medium heat. Sear in two batches, 2–3 minutes a side, just to colour it. Don''t push for deep browning or the paprika and garlic will burn. Set aside.', 'Crisp the chorizo. Fry in the same pot, stirring now and then, until it starts to brown and crisp, about 5 minutes.', 'Soften the vegetables. Add the onion and celery and cook until soft, 5–7 minutes.', 'Build the base. Stir in the garlic and rosemary for a minute, then the tomato purée, and cook until it darkens a shade, 2–3 minutes.', 'Add tomatoes and potatoes. Add the tomatoes, water and potatoes. Season lightly, bring to a simmer, cover and cook on low for 15 minutes, until the potatoes are just starting to soften.', 'Add beans and chicken. Stir in the beans, nestle the chicken back in skin-side up, cover and simmer for 25 minutes, until the chicken is cooked through and the potatoes are tender. Add a splash more water if it looks dry; take the lid off for the last 5 minutes if it''s too loose.', 'Finish. Off the heat, remove the rosemary stalks and stir in the parsley. Taste, adjust salt, and serve.']::text[], '40e72e027fa881dba8fcf17264683be2'),
('mums-chicken-curry', 'Mum''s Chicken Curry', '🍛', 'Meera Sodha''s family chicken curry: onions, ginger, garlic and chilli, tomatoes, yogurt and ground almonds. Serves 4, about 1 hour. Source: Made in India, p.100 (US edition — metric amounts below are converted)', 4, 60, 'Made in India, p.100 (US edition — metric amounts below are converted)', null, 'Serve with chapatis, naan or rice, and yogurt on the table.', array['Fry the onions. Heat the ghee and oil in a wide, lidded frying pan over medium heat. Add the cumin seeds and cinnamon, let them sizzle for a minute, then add the onions. Cook for 10–12 minutes, stirring now and then, until golden brown.', 'Make the paste. Meanwhile, pound the ginger, garlic and chillies with a pinch of salt in a mortar and pestle to a coarse paste.', 'Build the sauce. Add the paste to the pan and cook gently for 2 minutes. Pour in the passata and cook for a few minutes until thick and paste-like, then stir in the tomato purée, cumin, turmeric and ½ tsp salt.', 'Add yogurt and chicken. Whisk the yogurt and stir it in slowly. Once it bubbles, add the chicken, put the lid on and cook on a gentle heat for about 30 minutes.', 'Finish. Stir in the ground almonds and garam masala and cook for 5 more minutes.']::text[], '41372e027fa881099c63d74c79116e65'),
('spicy-pork-and-fennel-polpette', 'Spicy Pork & Fennel Polpette', '🍝', 'Pork meatballs with toasted fennel seed and chilli, roasted then poached in tomato sauce. Makes 30 balls (3–5 per person). About 30 minutes, plus the tomato sauce. Source: Polpo, p.153', null, 30, 'Polpo, p.153', null, 'Easy to halve: 750g pork, 2 small eggs (or 1 large + a splash of milk), 75g breadcrumbs, 10g fennel, 750ml sauce.', array['Heat the oven. 220°C / Gas 8.', 'Mix. Put the pork, eggs, breadcrumbs, chilli, fennel, salt and pepper in a large bowl and work it together well with your hands.', 'Roll and roast. Roll into 45g balls, put them on a greased baking tray and roast for 10 minutes, turning once, until they start to brown.', 'Poach. Simmer the meatballs in the tomato sauce in a saucepan for 10 minutes.', 'Serve. 3–5 balls each with some sauce and lightly toasted focaccia.']::text[], '41372e027fa8818bb9ced0886c263871'),
('spring-chicken-and-citrus-stew', 'Spring Chicken & Citrus Stew', '🍋', 'Whole chicken braised with fennel, leek, spring greens, olives and cannellini beans, finished with a lemon-and-egg sauce. From Apple Notes, saved 27 May 2025.', null, null, null, null, null, array['Joint the chicken into legs, thighs, breasts and wings.', 'Put a large casserole pan over a medium heat and add 3 tablespoons of olive oil. Season the chicken portions and place in the pan. Cook for 10 minutes, until browned on all sides, turning regularly, then remove to a plate.', 'Meanwhile, prep your veg. Peel and finely chop the garlic and onion. Trim and finely chop the fennel, trim and finely slice the leek and snap the woody ends off the asparagus. Pod the peas and broad beans, then pick the herb leaves and finely chop the stalks.', 'There should be a few tablespoons of fat left in the pan; if you have more, get rid of most of it. Add the garlic, onion, leek, fennel and herb stalks and cook until softened, stirring occasionally.', 'Place the chicken back in the pan, pour in the stock and season with black pepper. Cover with a lid, then cook over medium-low for around 45 minutes, or until the chicken is tender and falling off the bone.', 'Bring the mixture back up to a boil, de-stone and stir in the olives, peas, broad beans and cannellini beans. When the vegetables are done, add most of the herb leaves, reserving some for garnish.', 'Remove the chicken from the stew and use 2 forks to pull the meat off the bones. Discard the bones, return the meat to the pan, then season to perfection and remove from the heat.', 'At this point, the stew will be delicious, but it''s the next step that makes it amazing. Beat the juice of 2 lemons and the eggs together well, then pour slowly into the stew – don''t let it boil or the egg mixture with scramble – it should just add a gorgeous shine.', 'Stir in the yoghurt before ladling the stew into bowls. Drizzle a little extra virgin olive oil over each bowl, grate over a little Parmesan and serve sprinkled with the reserved herbs.']::text[], '41372e027fa881029d13d90470a79776'),
('green-smoothie', 'Green Smoothie', '🥬', 'From Apple Notes, saved 6 November 2024.', null, null, null, null, null, '{}', '41372e027fa881be8e0fc2163e320fb0'),
('kimchi', 'Kimchi', '🥢', 'Ingredient list only. From Apple Notes, saved 8 October 2023.', null, null, null, null, null, '{}', '41372e027fa881f6a378f28909c3a937'),
('lamb-vindaloo', 'Lamb Vindaloo', '🌶️', 'Lamb neck in a hot vindaloo sauce. Two-day recipe: the paste and sauce rest in the fridge overnight. From Apple Notes, saved 8 March 2019.', null, null, null, null, null, array['Blend the paste ingredients in a blender or bash using a pestle and mortar until smooth and combined. Rub half the paste into the lamb neck, cover and chill in the fridge overnight.', 'Heat most of the oil in a heavy-bottomed saucepan over a medium heat. Add the onion, garlic and ginger, cook slowly until the onion is translucent, then add the remaining curry paste and mix well. Cook your spices for a few mins, allowing the flavours to really open up. Decrease the heat, fold the potato into your mix, season well and cook for 5 mins.', 'Add the stock and simmer on a low heat until the potato is cooked, then lightly crush it. Add the chopped coriander, then remove from the heat and leave to cool. Chill in the fridge overnight.', 'The next day, heat the remaining oil in a frying pan. Season the lamb, brown it all over and cook until tender. Reheat the sauce, and add the lamb and any juices. Serve topped with coriander, with some wilted greens and fried red onion on the side.']::text[], '41372e027fa8817d8da9de5ff2d1f53f'),
('fast-and-loose-chicken-stew', 'Fast and Loose Chicken Stew', '🍲', 'Whole chicken simmered in a smoky tomato, caper and caraway sauce, pulled and served on rice. From Apple Notes, saved 6 February 2019.', null, null, null, null, null, array['Dice carrot, celery, onion, leek and capsicum. Finely chop chilli and garlic then add to a large saucepan with the olive oil.', 'Fry the vegetables over a med/low heat until softened then add the dry herbs & spices. Cook for a minute.', 'Add the tomatoes, capers and balsamic and reduce the heat to a simmer. Simmer for 5 min.', 'Divide the chicken into breasts, legs and wings and add all 6 pieces to the pan. Cover with gravy and simmer on a very low heat for 1.5 hours.', 'Remove the chicken from the pan and pull apart with a pair of forks and return to the pan. If there is still flesh on the bones and you plan on reheating this dish later, leave the bones in the stew for flavour.', 'Add salt to liking.', 'Add finely chopped parsley and serve on rice.']::text[], '41372e027fa881e8a1f4f19eee9fed35'),
('best-chilli-i-ever-made', 'Best Chilli I Ever Made', '🔥', 'I''ve been experimenting for a while and finally hit on a keeper. It''s got a serious kick to it but it''s not insanely hot. From Apple Notes, saved 1 September 2018.', null, null, null, null, '* Homemade beef tallow stock are key.
† Spice quantities are a guess, I didn''t measure but there was far more paprika than other spices.
Next time I''m gonna try adding oranges or juice or rind and/or lime juice for a fruity chilli sauce flavour.', array['In beef tallow, fry finely chopped vegetables and chilli until soft.', 'Gradually add beef and brown with salt and pepper.', 'Add spices, and continue frying for about 1 min.', 'Add tomatoes, stock, reduce heat to a simmer.', 'Grill peppers on both sides until skins are black, seal in a container to steam then remove skin, chop, add to pan with chopped jarred peppers.', 'Simmer for 1 hour and reduce until very thick.', 'Add kidney beans and a little water or more stock if necessary. Should be as thick as possible without being too dry.', 'Serve with guacamole and coriander.']::text[], '41372e027fa8818bb0c7c4f3bd83c4bf'),
('ramen', 'Ramen', '🍜', 'Ingredient lists for each component; no method in the original note. From Apple Notes, saved 18 August 2018.', null, null, null, null, null, '{}', '41372e027fa8816abee1ef5512792b1f'),
('rendang', 'Rendang', '🥥', 'Slow-cooked beef in a spiced coconut sauce, with toasted coconut and herby rice. About 1–1½ hours of simmering. From Apple Notes, saved 29 July 2018.', null, null, null, null, 'The original note''s method covers herby rice, deep-fried shallots and chilli, but their ingredients aren''t listed.', array['For the rendang paste, place the onion, ginger, galangal, garlic, lemongrass, turmeric and chillies into a food processor. Blend to form a smooth purée.', 'Heat a wok and add the oil. Fry the paste over a high heat until the paste turns darker and is highly aromatic.', 'Add the cardamom pods (crush the cardamom pods gently with the back of a spoon before frying) and the cinnamon stick broken in half and cook for another minute then add the meat.', 'Fry the meat in the paste stirring all the time, until it is well browned.', 'Pour over the coconut milk and tamarind purée and bring to a gentle simmer, add the kaffir lime leaves and zest.', 'Season with salt, stir well then reduce to a simmer.', 'Cook for between one hour and one hour and a half, stirring frequently during this time. The meat should be really tender and the sauce really reduced and rich.', 'Meanwhile toast the coconut flakes in a dry pan. Keep a close eye on this, as it will burn really quickly. It should be a lovely deep brown colour when ready. Blend to a powder in a small blender or pound in a pestle and mortar. When the rendang is ready stir into the mixture making sure it is well mixed in.', 'For the herby rice, wash the rice really well under cold water, the water will run clear when it is washed enough. Allow to drip dry. Heat the oil in a heavy-based saucepan that has a tight fitting lid.', 'Add the spices and fry for a minute.', 'Add the rice and fry until the grains begin to turn an opaque white.', 'Add the water and a good pinch of salt.', 'Bring to the boil then allow to bubble away until nearly all the water has been absorbed and air holes are beginning to appear.', 'At this point pop the lid on. Leave the heat on underneath for a minute (this will create lots of steam) then allow to stand for five minutes (do not remove the lid).', 'Keep warm until everything else is ready. You can hold rice like this for up to half an hour.', 'To serve, mix the herbs into the rice and divide between four warmed serving dishes. Next spoon on the rendang, finish with a few sprigs of coriander and serve immediately, garnished with the deep-fried shallots and chilli.']::text[], '41372e027fa8814584e3ee4083fde933'),
('southern-style-sausage-stew', 'Southern-Style Sausage Stew', '🌭', 'Pork sausages in a smoky tomato sauce with peppers and celery, served with brown rice. From Apple Notes, saved 12 June 2018.', null, null, null, null, null, array['Fill a medium pan with salted water, place on a high heat and bring to the boil.', 'Heat a splash of oil in a medium deep sided pan on a medium heat. Prick the sausages with a fork and cook for 10 minutes, or until browned on all sides, turning regularly.', 'Meanwhile, peel and trim the onion and celery. Halve and deseed the pepper, removing the core and stalk, then chop everything into 1cm chunks. Peel, crush and roughly chop the garlic.', 'Add the rice to the boiling water and cook for 25 minutes, or until tender. Drain in a sieve, then tip back into the warm pan. Cover with a lid and set aside off the heat.', 'When the sausages are nicely browned, remove from the pan. There should be a little fat left at the bottom of the pan. Add enough oil to make this up to 1 tablespoon, then add the vegetables, garlic and paprika.', 'Halve, deseed and finely chop the chilli, then add to the pan. Cover with a lid and cook on a medium heat for 10 to 15 minutes, or until soft.', 'Meanwhile, boil the kettle. Dissolve the stock cube in a jug with 350ml of boiling water. Roughly chop the tomatoes and put to one side.', 'Sprinkle the flour over the veg, then stir before adding the tomatoes, vinegar, sausages and stock, then bring to the boil.', 'Squash the tomatoes into the sauce with a wooden spoon, then simmer for 10 minutes, or until you have a nice thick sauce around the sausages.', 'Meanwhile, trim and finely slice the spring onions. When the stew is ready, taste and season with sea salt and black pepper. Divide the stew and rice between plates, scatter with spring onions, then serve.']::text[], '41372e027fa881e8b4edf9bcb63e23aa'),
('moroccan-lamb-soup', 'Moroccan Lamb Soup', '🥣', 'Ingredient list only. From Apple Notes, saved 7 February 2018.', null, null, null, null, null, '{}', '41372e027fa881298360d5c8ed5f7aeb'),
('beef-liver', 'Beef Liver', '🥩', 'Beef liver braised with mushrooms, onion, chilli, thyme and white wine. From Apple Notes, saved 20 September 2016.', null, null, null, null, null, array['Put the liver slices in salty water for about 2 hours. This will expel the excess blood that liver usually contains.', 'Dry the liver and set it aside.', 'Put the onion in a pan and the mushrooms, the chillies, and add the olive oil.', 'Heat the oil.', 'Cook them gently in a very slow fire for 10 minutes.', 'Add the liver slices and set the fire in medium level. Cook it from both sides for 10 minutes. Add salt, pepper, sugar and herbs. Mix all well.', 'Add the wine and keep the fire in same level. Add ½ glass of water and cook till the sauce that has been created will be thicken.', 'Serve hot.']::text[], '41372e027fa881738860d867a9856a2e'),
('chargrilled-tuna-with-dressed-beans-and-loads-of-herbs', 'Chargrilled Tuna with Dressed Beans and Loads of Herbs', '🐟', '"Loaded with herbs and beautiful beans, this grilled tuna steak is an absolutely gorgeous summer dish." Serves 4 | 5 min plus soaking | Super easy. From Apple Notes, saved 20 September 2016.', 4, null, null, null, null, array['This is a really beautiful summer dish that takes hardly any time at all to prepare – and, of course, you can use different kinds of beans, such as black-eyed beans, flageolets, butter beans or even lentils. It''s a terrific hot snack or main dish – and with the tuna all torn up it makes a great salad. I like to griddle the tuna for a minute on each side so it has colour on the outside but still remains a little pink in the middle. A lot of people still want to cook tuna all the way through (which I think is madness!), but you must do it how you like best. It''s worth seeing if you prefer it pink in the middle though. I''m really going heavy on the herbs in this recipe – 4 or 5 years ago this might have been a bit OTT, but it''s really easy to get hold of a good selection of herbs now, so the more the merrier.', 'Feel free to use a couple of tins of beans if it''s more convenient. Tinned beans aren''t bad these days – they have got much better for some reason. But if you''re using dried, which still taste better, soak them overnight in water. They''ll double in size. You then just need to drain them and put them into a pan with fresh water to cover. Bring to the boil, then simmer them for around 40 minutes or until tender – sometimes I put a squashed tomato and a potato in the water with them, as it helps to soften the skins. When done, drain them, discarding the tomato and potato, and put them into a large bowl with 8 tablespoons of peppery olive oil, the red onion, anchovies and chillies. Season with salt and pepper and the lemon juice – for a bit of a twang.', 'Preheat your griddle pan until really hot. Season the tuna steaks with salt and pepper, sprinkle over the lemon zest and pat a little olive oil on both sides. Sear the steaks for a minute on each side. While the fish is searing, get your guests round the table. Throw the herbs into the dressed beans, mix up and divide between the plates. Take the tuna off the heat, tear it up and place on top of the warm beans. Nice with some cold white wine.']::text[], '41372e027fa88149bed2ecfdbbb6d0ed'),
('omega-3-and-couscous', 'Omega 3 and Couscous', '🐟', 'Red mullet or sardines steamed over couscous with fennel, tomatoes and anchovies, served with lemony yoghurt. From Apple Notes, saved 14 September 2016.', null, null, null, null, null, array['Silly name, but what a great dish! Omega 3 refers to all the lovely goodness that you get from oily fish like red mullet, sardines and fresh anchovies, so do feel free to use any one of these, or a mixture of them. This is also great served with linguine pasta instead of couscous.', 'First of all, lay your fish out in one layer on your worktop to give you an idea of how much you are dealing with. Next get yourself a pan with a lid – ideally one that''s the right size for the fish to be spread out in one layer. This is so that it can all cook at the same time. Put your pan on the heat, add 4 or 5 tablespoons of olive oil, and slowly fry your onions, fennel, chilli, fennel seeds and bay leaf with the lid on until nice and softened. This should take about 10 minutes.', 'Meanwhile put your couscous in a bowl and just cover it with salted, boiling water. Put to one side to soak for about 5 minutes. When the onions are sweet and soft, add the tomatoes and anchovies, stir together and carefully season to taste. Shake the pan so that the onions and tomatoes cover the bottom of the pan evenly. Dress the couscous lightly with a little olive oil and the juice and zest of one of the lemons. Sprinkle the couscous over the top of the onions and tomatoes in one even layer. Then place the fish over the top of that and finish off with a drizzle of olive oil. Place the lid on top and simmer slowly on the hob for about 12 minutes.', 'Meanwhile, season the yoghurt with salt, pepper and the remaining lemon juice and sprinkle over the reserved fennel tops and mint. Serve the pan of fish in the middle of the table with a bowl of yoghurt and let everyone help themselves. Lightly stir the fish up, check the seasoning and eat straight away.']::text[], '41372e027fa881b7af89cc60a251162a'),
('indian-lamb-stew-with-spinach', 'Indian Lamb Stew with Spinach', '🍛', 'Lamb or goat slow-cooked in the oven with tomato, ginger and chilli, topped with garlicky greens. About 3½ hours. From Apple Notes, saved 12 September 2016.', null, 210, null, null, null, array['Preheat the oven to 275°F (about 135°C).', 'In a large Dutch oven, warm the ghee over medium heat.', 'Add the lamb and sauté until browned on all sides.', 'Add the onions, garlic, ginger, tomatoes, chili powder, salt, turmeric, and jalapeño and continue cooking for 10 minutes, until the onions are soft.', 'Add enough water to just cover the meat.', 'Cover the pot and place in the oven for 3 hours.', 'Remove from the oven and adjust the seasoning, if needed. Set aside.', 'To make the topping, slice the spinach into ¼-inch-wide strips.', 'Heat the ghee in a skillet over medium heat. Add the garlic and greens and sauté for just a few minutes, until bright green. Remove from the heat and stir in the lime juice.', 'To serve, ladle a serving of the stew into a bowl. Top with some of the sautéed greens and, if you wish, a tablespoon of yogurt.']::text[], '41372e027fa881d4b705f842835e318b'),
('salmon-and-pesto-dressed-veg', 'Salmon & Pesto-Dressed Veg', '🌿', 'Pan-fried salmon with new potatoes, green beans and broccoli tossed in homemade pesto. From Apple Notes, saved 9 September 2016.', null, null, null, null, 'Pesto is fantastic – everyone likes it. As well as dressing vegetables, try it with pasta, roast meats on hot toasts, or drizzled over soups. Just give it a bash!', array['To make the pesto, place a small frying pan over a medium heat, tip in the pine nuts and toast until very lightly golden – keep them moving so they don''t burn, then place in a small bowl and put to one side.', 'Peel the garlic, then place in a pestle and mortar with a pinch of sea salt.', 'Pick and tear in the basil leaves.', 'Bash the mixture to a paste, then add the pine nuts and pound again, leaving a little bit of texture. Scrape the mixture into the small bowl.', 'Add 2 to 3 tablespoons of extra virgin olive oil – you need just enough to bind the pesto and give it an oozy consistency – then finely grate and stir through the Parmesan.', 'Add a squeeze of lemon juice. Have a taste and season with a pinch of black pepper and a squeeze more lemon juice, if you think it needs it.', 'Scrub the potatoes well, then trim the beans and broccoli.', 'Fill a large pan three quarters of the way up with boiling water, add a pinch of salt and bring to the boil.', 'Once boiling, carefully add the potatoes and cook for 15 minutes, adding the beans and the broccoli for the final 5 minutes.', 'Meanwhile, heat a large non-stick frying pan over a high heat.', 'Rub the salmon fillets all over with olive oil and season with salt and pepper.', 'Place in the hot pan, skin-side down, turn the heat down to medium and cook for 4 minutes, or until golden underneath.', 'Use a fish slice to turn them over, then cook the fillets for a further 2 to 3 minutes, or until just cooked through.', 'Remove the pan from the heat, rest for 30 seconds, then add a good squeeze of lemon juice and give the pan a good shake.', 'Drain the vegetables well, then tip into a large bowl. Add the pesto, then use tongs to coat everything nicely.', 'Divide the fish fillets and vegetables between your plates, drizzle over the juices from the pan, then serve with lemon wedges for squeezing over.']::text[], '41372e027fa8817f931be99217bc78ac'),
('braised-greens', 'Braised Greens', '🥬', 'Silverbeet and chicory with garlic oil, finished with rocket and lemon. From Apple Notes, saved 9 September 2016.', null, null, null, null, null, array['This is a really simple and tasty way to make greens less boring. Also, having a mixture of greens makes things much more appealing. I''ve used three types of greens, including rocket, which is always thought of as a salad leaf.', 'Chicory is a slightly bitter-tasting green leaf which is excellent for braising. If you can''t get hold of it then try substituting with any other type of green, like spinach, cabbage, cavolo nero, Chinese leaf, beet leaves, sprouting broccoli – whatever you fancy.', 'Half fill a large pot with salted water, bring to the boil and add your silverbeet and chicory. Cook for 2 or 3 minutes until the greens are tender, or al dente, then drain in a colander.', 'To your empty pan add 4 large lugs of olive oil and the garlic. Fry the garlic until lightly coloured, then throw in your cooked chicory and silverbeet. Season and stir around to coat in all the lovely flavoured oil.', 'After 1 minute, remove from the heat, add the rocket and squeeze in the lemon juice. Stir once more, check the seasoning again, and serve immediately. Great with grilled meats or scallops, or even served cold on an antipasti plate.']::text[], '41372e027fa881678e73ffb5c0823698'),
('unbelievable-root-vegetable-salad', 'Unbelievable Root Vegetable Salad', '🥕', 'Shaved beetroot, carrot, celery, radicchio, radish and fennel in a grilled chilli dressing. From Apple Notes, saved 9 September 2016.', null, null, null, null, 'In the original note, "grilled chilli dressing" was a link to a separate recipe, which wasn''t copied over.', array['Very finely slice or shave the beetroots and carrots, using good knife skills, a mandolin on a fine setting or, most easily, a speed peeler, until you have a pile of thin, wavy, crunchy slices. Place in a big mixing bowl. With a sharp knife, slice the celery heart and leaves, the radicchio, the radishes and the fennel as finely as you can and add to the bowl of root veg slices.', 'Toss the root vegetables in the grilled chilli dressing, season carefully to taste, and serve sprinkled with the reserved fennel tops.']::text[], '41372e027fa88162adbdc376bd8674d9'),
('roasted-baby-leeks-with-thyme', 'Roasted Baby Leeks with Thyme', '🧅', 'Blanched baby leeks roasted with olive oil, red wine vinegar, thyme and garlic. From Apple Notes, saved 9 September 2016.', null, null, null, null, null, array['Preheat your oven to 200°C/400°F/gas 6. I like to serve 4 or 5 baby leeks per person, depending on their size. Lightly trim both ends and peel back the first or second layer of leaves and discard. Drop the leeks in a pan of boiling salted water for 2 to 3 minutes to soften – this is called blanching. Drain them well (if there''s too much water in them they won''t roast properly) and toss in a bowl with a good lug of olive oil, a splash of red wine vinegar, the chopped thyme leaves and the garlic. Arrange the leeks in one layer in a baking tray or earthenware dish and roast in the preheated oven for about 10 minutes until golden and almost caramelized. Keep your eye on them – I''ve seen many chefs burn baby leeks when cooking them this way and it drives me mad!']::text[], '41372e027fa881ab831bd2daadd43fc0'),
('turkish-style-couscous', 'Turkish-Style Couscous', '🍅', 'Spiced couscous with chopped veg, chilli, lots of herbs, tomato purée and lemon. From Apple Notes, saved 9 September 2016.', null, null, null, null, null, array['Place the couscous, cumin, paprika and a big pinch of salt into a bowl. Stir to combine, then pour over just enough boiling water to cover the couscous. Cover the bowl and leave for about 10 minutes.', 'Meanwhile, finely chop the onion, cucumber, tomatoes and chilli (I leave the seeds in, but deseed if you prefer). Finely chop the mint and coriander leaves, and the parsley (including the stalks).', 'Mix the couscous up with a fork, then stir in the tomato purée until well coated (I do this with my hands – it''s messy, but quite therapeutic!). Add all the chopped vegetables, chilli and herbs and mix well. Stir in the oil and the lemon zest and juice, then season to how you like it. Serve as a side with grilled meat or fish or eat it on its own if you want a light meal or snack.']::text[], '41372e027fa881cba54def076d3143ff'),
('pumpkin-chickpea-and-coconut-curry', 'Pumpkin, Chickpea & Coconut Curry', '🎃', 'Pumpkin or squash and chickpeas simmered in coconut milk with mustard seeds and curry leaves. About 1 hour. From Apple Notes, saved 9 September 2016.', null, 60, null, null, null, array['Chop the pumpkin or squash into 3cm chunks and cut the ginger into matchsticks. Pick the coriander leaves and finely chop the stalks.', 'Pour a good lug of groundnut oil into a large saucepan and place on a high heat. Add the ginger, garlic, red chilli and shallots, then reduce to a medium heat. Cook until golden, stirring occasionally, then add the mustard seeds, curry leaves, and coriander stalks and fry until the curry leaves go crispy. Add the turmeric, tomatoes and coconut milk. Bring to the boil, then add the pumpkin and chickpeas. Reduce to a low heat, cover with a lid and simmer for 45 minutes. Check occasionally and add a splash of water if it looks a bit dry.', 'When the time''s up, take the lid off and cook for a further 15 minutes or so until the sauce is lovely and thick.', 'Scatter with coriander leaves and serve with rice, naan bread and chutneys and dips on the side.']::text[], '41372e027fa8818c807eedc51ca25230'),
('thai-style-mussels', 'Thai-Style Mussels', '🦪', 'Mussels steamed in coconut milk with lemongrass, chilli and fish sauce. About 15 minutes. From Apple Notes, saved 9 September 2016.', null, 15, null, null, null, array['Wash the mussels thoroughly, discarding any that aren''t tightly closed.', 'Trim and finely slice the spring onions, peel and finely slice the garlic. Pick and set aside the coriander leaves, then finely chop the stalks. Cut the lemongrass into 4 pieces, then finely slice the chilli.', 'In a wide saucepan, heat a little groundnut oil and soften the spring onion, garlic, coriander stalks, lemongrass and most of the red chilli for around 5 minutes.', 'Add the coconut milk and fish sauce and bring to the boil, then add the mussels and cover the pan.', 'Steam the mussels for 5 minutes, or until they''ve all opened and are cooked. Discard any unopened mussels.', 'Finish with a squeeze of lime juice, then sprinkle with coriander leaves and the remaining chilli to serve.']::text[], '41372e027fa8816db174c88f6c78b027'),
('sausage-broccoli-and-chilli-pasta', 'Sausage, Broccoli & Chilli Pasta', '🍝', 'Crispy sausagemeat, broccoli and rigatoni with chilli, garlic, lemon and a spoonful of crème fraîche. Serves 4, about 25 minutes. Source: Sausage, broccoli & chilli pasta – BBC Good Food', 4, 25, 'Sausage, broccoli & chilli pasta – BBC Good Food', 'https://www.bbcgoodfoodme.com/recipes/sausage-broccoli-chilli-pasta/', 'Works with any pasta. Wholewheat spaghetti is fine; it takes a couple of minutes longer.
The red chilli is optional if you''d rather rely on the chilli flakes.
Check you have enough: garlic.
Pick up at the corner shop: broccoli (about 330g).', array['Cook the pasta and broccoli. Boil the rigatoni in salted water, adding the broccoli for the last 3–4 minutes. Keep a splash of the cooking water before you drain.', 'Crisp the sausagemeat. Put the sausagemeat and oil in a cold frying pan, then fry over medium heat, breaking it up, until golden and crisp. Lift out with a slotted spoon.', 'Cook the flavourings. Fry the chilli flakes, garlic and parsley stalks in the same pan for a minute. Add the lemon zest and put the sausagemeat back in.', 'Bring it together. Toss in the pasta and broccoli, then stir through the crème fraîche, lemon juice and a splash of the pasta water.', 'Serve. Top with the parsley leaves and sliced red chilli.']::text[], '41372e027fa88135af70c208ff76faec'),
('fish-pie-pasta-bake', 'Fish Pie Pasta Bake', '🐟', 'Macaroni and fish pie mix in a creamy mustard and spinach sauce, topped with Cheddar and grilled, with a chicory and rocket salad. Serves 4, about 20 minutes. Source: Fish pie pasta bake – Jamie Oliver (from 20-Minute Meals: Real Fast Food)', 4, 20, 'Fish pie pasta bake – Jamie Oliver (from 20-Minute Meals: Real Fast Food)', 'https://www.jamieoliver.com/recipes/fish/fish-pie-pasta-bake/', 'No microwave needed: defrost the spinach overnight in the fridge, or warm it in a covered pan for a few minutes.
Your frying pan needs to be grill-safe; if not, tip everything into a baking dish before grilling.
From your Pantry you''ll likely need: macaroni, English mustard, fish pie mix, Cheddar (Out), plain flour (you stock wholemeal), chicory and rocket. You have onions, spinach, UHT milk, olive oil and red wine vinegar.', array['Get set up. Heat the grill to high and boil the kettle. Defrost the spinach.', 'Soften the onion. Finely chop the onion and cook it in a large ovenproof frying pan with the olive oil on a high heat for 5 minutes, stirring often.', 'Cook the pasta. Boil the macaroni in salted water for 8 minutes, then drain, keeping a mug of the cooking water.', 'Make the sauce. Stir the flour and mustard into the onions, then slowly stir in the milk. Add the spinach with its liquid and season.', 'Add the fish. Stir in the fish and simmer for 5 minutes on a medium heat. Mix in the pasta, adding a splash of the cooking water if it''s thick.', 'Cheese and grill. Grate in half the Cheddar, grate the rest on top, then put the pan on the top shelf under the grill for a few minutes until golden and bubbling.', 'Make the salad. Slice the chicory bases and separate the leaves. Toss with the rocket, vinegar and extra virgin olive oil, season, and serve with the bake.']::text[], '41472e027fa8819496dbc2b0740a8667'),
('easy-oven-baked-fish-with-tomatoes', 'Easy Oven Baked Fish with Tomatoes', '🐟', 'White fish baked under garlicky, oregano-spiced tinned tomatoes, served with couscous and wilted spinach. Serves 2 (with 2 fillets), about 25 minutes. Source: Easy Oven Baked Fish with Tomatoes – Budget Bytes', 2, 25, 'Easy Oven Baked Fish with Tomatoes – Budget Bytes', 'https://www.budgetbytes.com/easy-oven-baked-fish-with-tomatoes/', 'The original serves 4 with 450g of fish and the same amount of tomato mix, so this scales up without changing anything else.
The original uses fire-roasted tinned tomatoes; plain chopped tomatoes work fine.
Couscous and spinach are my serving suggestion, not part of the original recipe.
Check you have enough: oregano (marked Low in the Pantry when saved).', array['Heat the oven. Set it to 200°C.', 'Mix the tomatoes. Stir together the drained tomatoes, garlic, olive oil, oregano, salt, pepper and lemon juice.', 'Layer the dish. Spread about a third of the tomato mix in a baking dish that holds the fish in one layer. Lay the fillets on top and spoon the rest over so the fish is covered.', 'Bake. Cook for about 15 minutes, until the fish flakes easily.', 'Make the sides. While it bakes, cover the couscous with boiling water and leave it for 5 minutes, then fluff with a fork. Wilt the spinach in a pan.', 'Serve. Scatter the parsley over the fish and serve with the couscous, spinach and the juices from the dish.']::text[], '41772e027fa88183ad65c25143f8b431'),
('beef-keema-with-sweet-potato-carrot-and-spinach', 'Beef Keema with Sweet Potato, Carrot & Spinach', '🍛', 'Spiced beef mince simmered with sweet potato and carrot, finished with spinach. Serves 4. About 45 minutes including brown rice. Based on: Indian beef keema with carrots & potatoes (BBC Good Food)', 4, 45, null, null, 'Changes from the original: sweet potato instead of potato (added later so it holds its shape), spinach added, a habanero for heat since the curry powder isn''t a hot one, and yoghurt and lemon in place of mango chutney (no added sugar).
Check you have enough: curry powder.
Keeps well for leftovers.', array['Start the rice. Brown rice takes 30–35 minutes, so get it going first.', 'Brown the mince. Fry the mince with a little of the oil and some seasoning in a large pan over high heat, breaking it up. Lift it out and pour off the fat.', 'Soften the aromatics. Add the rest of the oil and cook the onion, garlic, ginger and chilli for about 8 minutes until soft. Stir in the curry powder and carrots and cook for 2 minutes.', 'Simmer. Stir in the tomato purée, stock and mince. Simmer for 5 minutes, then add the sweet potato and simmer for about 15 minutes more, until tender.', 'Add the spinach. Stir it in until wilted. Check the seasoning.', 'Serve. Spoon over the rice and top with coriander, yoghurt and a squeeze of lemon.']::text[], '41772e027fa88104acc9c82df02b1f35'),
('pesto-spaghetti-with-salmon-broccoli-and-asparagus', 'Pesto Spaghetti with Salmon, Broccoli & Asparagus', '🍝', 'Wholewheat spaghetti, broccoli and asparagus tossed in homemade basil pesto, with pan-fried salmon flaked over the top. Serves 3, about 25 minutes. Based on: Salmon & Pesto-Dressed Veg', 3, 25, null, null, 'Changes from the original: spaghetti instead of new potatoes, asparagus instead of green beans, ordinary broccoli instead of tenderstem, pesto made in the blender, and the salmon flaked through so 2 fillets stretch to 3 portions.
The original pesto uses 50g basil. One bunch works; parsley makes up the difference.
Check you have enough: pine nuts, Parmesan, lemons (1 covers it).', array['Make the pesto. Toast the pine nuts in a dry pan until pale gold. Blend with the garlic, basil, oil and a pinch of salt, keeping some texture. Stir in the Parmesan by hand and add a squeeze of lemon. Taste before adding more salt.', 'Cook the pasta and veg. Boil the spaghetti in salted water. Add the broccoli and asparagus for the last 4 minutes. Keep a mug of the cooking water, then drain.', 'Cook the salmon. Rub the fillets with oil, salt and pepper. Fry skin-side down over medium heat for 4 minutes, then turn and cook for 2–3 minutes more. Squeeze over some lemon.', 'Bring it together. Toss the pasta and veg with the pesto and a splash of the pasta water. Flake the salmon over the top and finish with a little more Parmesan.']::text[], '41872e027fa88195bdabcdf544dd0dd9')
